## 1. `server/room.ts:662` — persistence trigger — **TRUE**

Persistence is dirty-flag gated. `private dirtySinceStore = false;` (`server/room.ts:185`), reset in `persist()`:

```ts
// server/room.ts:662-671
async persist(): Promise<void> {
  const save = this.composeSave();
  ...
  await this.persistence.storeWorld(this.worldId, blob, save.version);
  this.dirtySinceStore = false;
}
```

**Exactly five sites set the flag** (this is the complete list):

| Line | Mutation |
|---|---|
| `room.ts:339` | dimension travel (`travelPlayer`) |
| `room.ts:389` | nether shard evict (`sweepNetherShard`) |
| `room.ts:461` | join |
| `room.ts:527` | leave |
| `room.ts:705` | block edits — **only** `blockPlaced` / `blockBroken` / `explosion` |

**Not marked:** inventory changes, health, hunger, effects, statistics, advancements, player movement/position, XP, mob state, containers, day clock, and `setDifficulty`/`setGameMode` (`room.ts:604-607` mutates every shard's engine, sets nothing).

Note `room.ts:705` filters *events*, not `blockChanges.drainEdits()` (`room.ts:704`) — block writes with no matching event (arrival-portal carving in `travelPlayer`, fire/fluid ticks) don't mark dirty via this path.

**Fire-and-forget: TRUE.**
```ts
// server/room.ts:784-786
if (this.tickCount % PERSIST_INTERVAL_TICKS === 0 && this.dirtySinceStore) {
  void this.persist();
}
```
`void`, no `await`, no in-flight guard. A `persist()` slower than 60s (`PERSIST_INTERVAL_TICKS = 20 * 60`, `room.ts:132`) overlaps itself; `storeWorld` (`server/persistence.ts:57-59`) is an unguarded `UPDATE`, so overlapping writes race last-write-wins.

**Is there an interval persist covering unmarked state? NO.** Line 784 is the only periodic persist and it is `&& this.dirtySinceStore`. Nothing persists unconditionally.

**Mitigating nuance the review should account for:** `leave()` (527) and `join()` (461) always mark dirty, and `shutdown()` (`room.ts:658`) *awaits* `persist()`. So the loss window is narrower than "health/inventory never persist": it's a **process crash / SIGKILL during a live session where the only changes since the last store were unmarked ones**. Graceful paths (idle evict, SIGTERM at `server/index.ts:132-134`) do capture them. Verdict on the flag list itself: exactly as claimed.

---

## 2. `lib/net/NetworkSession.ts:628` — unserialized inbound frames — **TRUE**

```ts
// lib/net/NetworkSession.ts:628-651
const onServerFrame = (data: unknown) => {
  traffic.inBytes += typeof data === "string" ? data.length : ((data as ArrayBuffer).byteLength ?? 0);
  if (simulatedLatencyMs > 0 || simulatedJitterMs > 0) {
    recvLine.push(() => void processServerFrame(data));
    return;
  }
  void processServerFrame(data);
};

async function processServerFrame(data: unknown): Promise<void> {
  if (typeof data !== "string") {
    const sync = await gunzipWorldSync(new Uint8Array(data as ArrayBuffer));   // ← yields
    if (sync) {
      if (sync.dimension !== state.dimension) return;
      applyWorldSync(sync);
      setStatus("online");
    }
    return;
  }
  const message = decodeServerFrame(data);   // ← text path: fully synchronous, no awaits
  ...
```

`void processServerFrame(...)` — launched, never awaited, no queue, no sequence gate. The text branch (`650`+, the whole `switch`) contains no `await` and runs to completion synchronously; the binary branch suspends at `639`.

**In the browser this genuinely reorders.** `gunzipWorldSync` (`lib/net/codec.ts:63-74`) takes the non-Bun path — `DecompressionStream` + `new Response(stream).arrayBuffer()` — which spans multiple macrotasks. A `tick` frame arriving during that window applies its `blocks` (`NetworkSession.ts:656` → `applyBlocks`), then the older snapshot lands on top and clobbers them. Under Bun (`codec.ts:68`, `gunzipSync`) only microtasks are involved, so ordering holds — this is a browser-only defect, which is where it matters.

The `delayLine` comment at `NetworkSession.ts:206-213` explicitly asserts FIFO because "the seq/journal handling downstream assumes order" — but `recvLine.push(() => void processServerFrame(data))` (`631`) only orders the *start* of processing, not completion. The invariant the comment claims is not actually upheld.

**Server-side trigger exists:** `room.ts:497` — `sink.send(await gzipWorldSync(...))` runs *after* `this.clients.set(claims.sub, conn)` at `room.ts:477`, so the tick loop can emit a tick frame to that conn before the worldSync is on the wire. `join` is otherwise protected only for travel via `pendingDimensionSync` (`room.ts:731`), which `join` never sets.

---

## 3. `NetworkSession.ts:282` / `:777` — double-close, no idempotency — **TRUE**

```ts
// lib/net/NetworkSession.ts:282-284
socket.onmessage = (frame) => onServerFrame(frame.data);
socket.onclose = (frame) => onSocketClosed(frame.code, frame.reason);
socket.onerror = () => onSocketClosed(1006, "socket error");
```

Both funnel into the same `onSocketClosed`. The **only** guard is `disposed` (`NetworkSession.ts:769`, set solely at `990`):

```ts
// lib/net/NetworkSession.ts:768-787
const onSocketClosed = (code: number, reason: string) => {
  if (disposed) return;
  if (FATAL_CLOSES.has(code) || !options.reconnect) { setStatus("closed", `${code} ${reason}`); return; }
  scheduleReconnect();
};

const scheduleReconnect = () => {
  if (disposed) return;
  if (reconnectAttempt >= RECONNECT_DELAYS_MS.length) { setStatus("closed", "could not reconnect"); return; }
  const delay = RECONNECT_DELAYS_MS[reconnectAttempt];
  reconnectAttempt += 1;
  setStatus("reconnecting", `attempt ${reconnectAttempt}`);
  reconnectTimer = setTimeout(() => void tryReconnectOnce(), delay);   // ← no guard on existing timer
};
```

**No flag, no connection generation counter, no in-flight check — confirmed.** Browsers fire `error` then `close` for an abnormal drop, so a single network failure calls `onSocketClosed` **twice** → `scheduleReconnect()` twice → `reconnectAttempt` burns 2 rungs of `RECONNECT_DELAYS_MS` per real failure (halving the ladder), and **`reconnectTimer` is overwritten at 786, orphaning the first timer** — it still fires. Two concurrent `tryReconnectOnce()` → two `options.reconnect()` ticket mints → two `handshake()` calls, each doing `ws = socket` (`NetworkSession.ts:265`), so one socket is orphaned with live handlers still bound to `onServerFrame`/`onSocketClosed`. Server-side, the second handshake's `join` closes the first with `CLOSE_KICKED` (`room.ts:449`) — which is in `FATAL_CLOSES` (`NetworkSession.ts:87`) — so the orphan's `onSocketClosed(4003)` drives the *live* session to `setStatus("closed")`. A transient drop can terminate a session that successfully reconnected.

`dispose` only clears the *current* `reconnectTimer` (`NetworkSession.ts:991`), so the orphaned timer survives disposal.

**At ~777: no reconnect timer guard — confirmed.**

---

## 4. `server/roomRegistry.ts:61` — evict-before-persist — **TRUE**

```ts
// server/roomRegistry.ts:61-68
async sweepIdle(): Promise<void> {
  for (const [worldId, room] of this.rooms) {
    if (room.playerCount() === 0 && room.emptySinceMs !== null && this.now() - room.emptySinceMs > IDLE_EVICT_MS) {
      this.rooms.delete(worldId);     // ← 64: removed from registry FIRST
      await room.shutdown();          // ← 65: persistence starts AFTER
    }
  }
}
```

`Room.shutdown()` (`room.ts:654-659`) ends in `await this.persist()`, and `persist` gzips the whole world (`room.ts:667-668`) then round-trips to Postgres (`persistence.ts:57-59`) — a multi-hundred-ms window on a large save.

**Yes, a new join creates a second room from stale DB state.** `getOrLoad` (`roomRegistry.ts:31-50`) checks `this.rooms` (32) and `this.loading` (34) — the entry is already gone from both, so it calls `this.persistence.loadWorld(worldId)` (41), reading the **pre-shutdown** blob. Two `Room` objects then exist for one `worldId`, and `storeWorld` has **no version guard** (unlike `putSaveBlob`'s stale-check predicate at `worldsService.ts:270-276`), so the loser is silently overwritten. Whichever lands last wins — typically the new room, discarding the evicted room's final state.

`shutdownAll` (`roomRegistry.ts:70-75`) has the same shape (`rooms.clear()` at 73, `await` at 74), but process exit follows, so it's benign. The `await` inside the `sweepIdle` loop also serializes evictions, widening the window for later rooms in the same sweep.

Fix shape: keep the room in `rooms` (or park the shutdown promise in `loading`) until `shutdown()` resolves, then delete.

---

## 5. `lib/online/worldsService.ts:168` — createWorld validation — **TRUE**

```ts
// lib/online/worldsService.ts:168-187
export async function createWorld(db: Db, userId: string, input: CreateWorldInput): Promise<{ ok: true; world: WorldSummary } | Failure> {
  const name = input.name?.trim();
  if (!name || name.length > 64) return fail("invalid");
  if (input.kind !== "sp-cloud" && input.kind !== "mp") return fail("invalid");
  if (!Number.isFinite(input.seed)) return fail("invalid");
  return db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    let profileId: string | null = null;
    if (input.profileId) {                       // ← 177: cap check is OPT-IN
      await tx.execute(lockAccount(userId));
      ...
      if (owned.length >= MAX_WORLDS_PER_PROFILE) return fail("conflict");
      profileId = input.profileId;
